      "abstract": "Historians have long identified Benjamin Urner as the first exponent of direct legislation in the United States, a claim that has been repeated without ever being carefully examined. In this article, I excavate Urner\u2019s career and political thought and show that while the oft-repeated account may give Urner too much credit as a direct democracy pioneer, it also neglects an important transatlantic socialist utopian strand in nineteenth-century radical political thought that shaped Urner\u2019s thought and played a critical role in the earliest efforts to promote direct democracy in both the United States and Europe. Direct legislation appealed to utopian socialists because it offered a way not only to enact desired economic change by combatting concentrated economic power but also to institutionalize the radical vision of democracy at the heart of utopian socialist thought.",
