      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Writing personal or collective history entails compiling archival evidence, tenuous traces of one\u2019s past life. It means to \u2018salvage something from time\u2019 (Annie Ernaux) and recover the present of past temporalities to uncover and clarify them. In this essay, I explore, in their relation to Woolf\u2019s literary and political representation of history, Woolf\u2019s photographic albums, that is a personal archive she compiled and organised throughout her life as \u2018the Stephen and Woolf family archivist\u2019 (Neverow) and historian. Through cultural theories of image (Warburg, Benjamin, Didi-Huberman), my aim is to see in what ways this bulk of factual documents provided Woolf with material for the writing of her personal memoir, her fiction (Night and Day, Mrs\u00a0Dalloway) and her essays (Three Guineas). I analyse how, relying on photography\u2019s inherent historicity, Woolf pictures historical gestures that subvert linear chronologies and patriarchal monumental rhetoric.",
